    passageText: `The tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle, also called the Krebs cycle or citric acid cycle, is a series of eight reactions occurring in the mitochondrial matrix that oxidizes acetyl-CoA (derived from pyruvate, fatty acids, and amino acids) to CO₂ while generating reducing equivalents NADH and FADH₂. Per turn: 1 GTP (or ATP), 3 NADH, 1 FADH₂, 2 CO₂. The key irreversible reaction is catalyzed by isocitrate dehydrogenase (converting isocitrate to α-ketoglutarate), which is allosterically activated by ADP and Ca²⁺ and inhibited by NADH.

Reducing equivalents generated by the TCA cycle and glycolysis feed into the electron transport chain (ETC) embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane. NADH donates electrons at Complex I (NADH:ubiquinone oxidoreductase); FADH₂ donates at Complex II (succinate dehydrogenase, the only TCA enzyme embedded in the inner membrane). Electrons flow through ubiquinone, Complex III (cytochrome bc1), cytochrome c, and Complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase), which reduces O₂ to H₂O. Complexes I, III, and IV pump protons from the matrix to the intermembrane space, generating the proton motive force (PMF).

ATP synthase (Complex V) uses the PMF to synthesize ATP (chemiosmosis). Rotenone blocks Complex I; antimycin A blocks Complex III; cyanide, CO, and azide block Complex IV. Oligomycin blocks ATP synthase. Uncouplers (e.g., DNP, thermogenin in brown adipose tissue) dissipate the proton gradient as heat without synthesizing ATP, increasing O₂ consumption while decreasing ATP production.`,
