    passageText: `Gene expression can be regulated at multiple levels, with transcriptional control being the most common. In prokaryotes, the lac operon (Jacob and Monod, 1961) became the paradigm for understanding gene regulation. The lac operon encodes genes for lactose metabolism (lacZ, lacY, lacA). In the absence of lactose, the lac repressor protein (encoded by lacI) binds the operator sequence, blocking RNA polymerase. When lactose (converted to allolactose) binds the repressor, the repressor undergoes conformational change, dissociates from the operator, and allows transcription. Additionally, when glucose is absent, elevated cAMP activates CAP (catabolite activator protein), which binds upstream of the lac promoter and stimulates transcription — providing positive control through catabolite repression.

Eukaryotic gene regulation is considerably more complex. Transcription factors (TFs) bind specific DNA sequences (enhancers, silencers, promoter-proximal elements) and recruit the transcriptional machinery or modulate chromatin accessibility. Activator TFs recruit coactivators (Mediator complex, histone acetyltransferases) that open chromatin and facilitate RNA Pol II initiation. Repressor TFs recruit histone deacetylases (HDACs) or DNA methyltransferases, condensing chromatin and silencing gene expression.

Steroid hormones illustrate a class of eukaryotic TFs (nuclear receptors) that signal directly to the nucleus. Cortisol diffuses across the cell membrane, binds the glucocorticoid receptor (GR) in the cytoplasm, causing dissociation of HSP90, nuclear localization of the GR-cortisol complex, dimerization, and binding to glucocorticoid response elements (GREs) in target gene promoters. This activates transcription of genes promoting gluconeogenesis and anti-inflammatory responses.`,
