    passageText: `The action potential (AP) is the fundamental electrical signal in excitable cells (neurons, muscle cells). At rest, the neuronal membrane potential is approximately −70 mV (inside negative), maintained by the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase and the differential permeability of the membrane to K⁺ (high) and Na⁺ (low). The Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase actively pumps 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in per ATP consumed, contributing to the resting negative potential and the concentration gradients (Na⁺ high outside; K⁺ high inside).

Depolarization occurs when the membrane potential reaches the threshold (~−55 mV), triggering rapid opening of voltage-gated Na⁺ channels (VGSCs). Na⁺ flows in down its electrochemical gradient, rapidly depolarizing the membrane to approximately +30 to +40 mV (overshoot). VGSCs then rapidly inactivate (the inactivation gate closes, even while the activation gate remains open). Voltage-gated K⁺ channels (delayed rectifiers) open more slowly, allowing K⁺ efflux that repolarizes the membrane. K⁺ channels remain open briefly after repolarization, causing hyperpolarization (afterhyperpolarization) below resting potential, before closing. During the absolute refractory period (VGSC inactivation gates closed), no new AP can be generated regardless of stimulus strength; during the relative refractory period (channels recovering), only superthreshold stimuli can trigger an AP.

Myelination dramatically increases AP conduction velocity. Myelin (formed by Schwann cells in the PNS and oligodendrocytes in the CNS) electrically insulates the axon; APs regenerate only at nodes of Ranvier (unmyelinated gaps), producing saltatory conduction. Demyelination in multiple sclerosis (MS) disrupts saltatory conduction, slowing or blocking AP propagation and producing the neurological deficits characteristic of MS.`,
