    passageText: `The polymerase chain reaction (PCR) amplifies specific DNA sequences exponentially using repeated cycles of denaturation, annealing, and extension. A typical PCR reaction contains: template DNA, two primers flanking the target region, heat-stable DNA polymerase (Taq polymerase, from Thermus aquaticus), dNTPs, and buffer. Each cycle consists of: (1) denaturation (~95°C, separates double-stranded DNA); (2) annealing (~50–65°C, primers bind to complementary sequences); (3) extension (~72°C, Taq polymerase synthesizes new DNA from primers). After n cycles, the target region is amplified 2ⁿ-fold. Real-time PCR (qPCR) quantifies the initial template amount by measuring fluorescence in each cycle; the cycle threshold (Ct) value is inversely proportional to initial template quantity.

Gel electrophoresis separates nucleic acids or proteins by size and charge. DNA fragments (negatively charged due to phosphate backbone) migrate through an agarose gel toward the positive electrode under an electric field. Smaller fragments migrate faster. A DNA ladder (size marker) of fragments of known sizes is run alongside samples to estimate fragment sizes by comparison.

Restriction enzymes (restriction endonucleases) cut double-stranded DNA at specific palindromic sequences (restriction sites). Restriction fragment length polymorphism (RFLP) analysis uses differences in restriction site locations between individuals to identify genetic variants. Southern blotting transfers gel-separated DNA fragments to a membrane for hybridization with a labeled probe, allowing detection of specific sequences. A related technique for RNA is Northern blotting; for proteins, Western blotting uses antibodies as probes.`,
